1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,530
Well, it's time to get back to it and finish what we started.

2
00:00:03,620 --> 00:00:09,380
Our arcade game is fun, but it's missing that crucial element that takes you from being a kind of fun

3
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:11,180
game to a really fun game.

4
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:14,330
And this is what polishing is all about.

5
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:16,460
It's the final stage of our workflow.

6
00:00:16,460 --> 00:00:21,530
So for the first step of polishing, we are going to make our graphics a bit better, make our game

7
00:00:21,530 --> 00:00:23,000
feel much more real.

8
00:00:23,390 --> 00:00:25,730
The first thing we want to discuss is layers.

9
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:29,660
We briefly discussed this in an earlier lesson, but let's run over it again.

10
00:00:29,870 --> 00:00:35,930
So in scratch, each of our sprites appear on our screen with an x and Y position, as you know.

11
00:00:36,110 --> 00:00:45,320
Now this is easy to see, but our sprites also have a z axis, which we can't easily see as everything

12
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:47,930
is 2D and we are looking from a top down view.

13
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:52,100
We basically the z axis is the layer.

14
00:00:52,430 --> 00:00:54,980
So imagine a stack of papers.

15
00:00:54,980 --> 00:00:59,300
You might have 20 pieces of paper on top of one another and each has its layer.

16
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:06,220
So if the paper is on the top of the pile, it is at layer one, and if it's on the bottom, it's layer

17
00:01:06,220 --> 00:01:06,880
20.

18
00:01:07,150 --> 00:01:13,150
And naturally, if a paper is at a higher layer like layer one, it will cover the paper that is at

19
00:01:13,150 --> 00:01:14,740
a lower layer like layer two.

20
00:01:14,770 --> 00:01:20,260
So the exact same thing happens in scratch when our sprites are on different layers.

21
00:01:20,290 --> 00:01:27,160
So if we play our game quickly and if we crash into a tree, you will see that, well, we kind of ski

22
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:29,380
over the tree, which doesn't make sense.

23
00:01:29,410 --> 00:01:31,630
So how do we fix this?

24
00:01:32,270 --> 00:01:39,230
Well, if we move over to our player sprite, we can grab another when flag clicked and then move up

25
00:01:39,230 --> 00:01:40,790
to the looks tab.

26
00:01:42,050 --> 00:01:43,790
Here we will see we have two options.

27
00:01:43,790 --> 00:01:51,740
Either we can make it go to the front or back, or we can have it go backwards or forwards a layer.

28
00:01:52,480 --> 00:01:55,960
So if we were to say go to back and then hit play.

29
00:01:56,260 --> 00:02:01,420
As you can see, our snow trail goes in front of us, so this isn't an option.

30
00:02:01,870 --> 00:02:04,120
So let's bring this back to the front.

31
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:05,050
Like this.

32
00:02:05,540 --> 00:02:09,350
Now, this time we can instead say go back one layer.

33
00:02:10,060 --> 00:02:15,160
So now, rather than sending this to the very back, we'll just moving it back one layer, which means

34
00:02:15,160 --> 00:02:17,470
our player sprite will be in layer two.

35
00:02:18,770 --> 00:02:24,230
Now dragging the script over to both our snow pixels, we can have it go backwards two layers, and

36
00:02:24,230 --> 00:02:27,020
we can also do the same for the other sprite.

37
00:02:27,970 --> 00:02:28,990
Clicking play.

38
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:31,060
Well that's weird.

39
00:02:31,480 --> 00:02:33,040
Our picks are listed in front.

40
00:02:33,190 --> 00:02:33,490
Hmm.

41
00:02:34,060 --> 00:02:36,100
Any ideas what's happening here?

42
00:02:37,110 --> 00:02:42,030
Well, the reason for this is because each time we start the game, it keeps going back more and more

43
00:02:42,030 --> 00:02:42,840
layers.

44
00:02:43,230 --> 00:02:51,090
So what we need to do is go to each of these sprites and bring in this go to front layer, just like

45
00:02:51,090 --> 00:02:51,540
that.

46
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:55,110
And we can also do the same for our tree sprite.

47
00:02:55,620 --> 00:03:00,660
We can just drag in a when flag clicked and then a go to front layer.

48
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:07,810
So now this way, when we start our game, each of our sprites starts on the same layer, but our player

49
00:03:07,810 --> 00:03:11,200
moves back one and our pixels go back two.

50
00:03:11,500 --> 00:03:12,820
So trying this out.

51
00:03:13,590 --> 00:03:15,360
As you can see, it works perfectly.

52
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:18,510
When we crash into a tree, we don't glide in front of it.

53
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:20,580
Now that's much more realistic.

54
00:03:22,130 --> 00:03:26,060
So with that out of the way, there is one more thing we need to do.

55
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:28,110
When we play the game.

56
00:03:28,110 --> 00:03:32,550
As you can see, our trees kind of just pop into view, which is a bit jarring.

57
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:39,380
Wouldn't it be better if they kind of faded in, almost like there was a mist and they were coming into

58
00:03:39,380 --> 00:03:40,610
view from the mist?

59
00:03:41,630 --> 00:03:46,430
Sounds great, but it sounds like a rather complex task.

60
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:50,510
But fear not, because there is a fairly simple way to do this.

61
00:03:51,290 --> 00:03:57,230
If you look over at our looks group, you will see this change color or set color to.

62
00:03:58,220 --> 00:04:01,460
Dragging the set color effect into our player sprite.

63
00:04:01,730 --> 00:04:05,480
You'll see that if we click it, oh, nothing happens.

64
00:04:05,960 --> 00:04:14,330
But if we change this number to say, 40, our sprite changes color and it almost looks like an alien.

65
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:18,790
But this isn't what we want so we can set it back to zero.

66
00:04:19,540 --> 00:04:24,670
But you will see that if we click here, there are all these other options.

67
00:04:25,210 --> 00:04:28,510
And the effect we are interested in is this ghost effect.

68
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:36,670
So let's set it to 95 and clicking it you will see how sprite almost fades out.

69
00:04:36,670 --> 00:04:39,130
So we can use this to fade in our trees.

70
00:04:39,370 --> 00:04:41,050
So quickly resetting it.

71
00:04:41,610 --> 00:04:44,850
We then can move over to our tree sprite.

72
00:04:45,690 --> 00:04:47,880
Now this is your next mini challenge.

73
00:04:47,880 --> 00:04:55,200
Try and see if you can create this effect where our trees fade into view instead of just suddenly appearing.

74
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:59,310
Come on, give it a try before we show you how to do it.

75
00:05:00,590 --> 00:05:01,790
Did you get it?

76
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:03,890
Now let's see how it's done.

77
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:11,540
Now, what we want to happen is when our sprite starts as a clone, we want to set the ghost effect

78
00:05:11,540 --> 00:05:12,590
to 95.

79
00:05:13,070 --> 00:05:20,090
Then to make it fade in, what we are going to do is grab this change effect by block and again set

80
00:05:20,090 --> 00:05:20,990
it to ghost.

81
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:22,120
Here.

82
00:05:22,120 --> 00:05:28,210
We want to drag it into this forever loop so that the ghosting slowly fades out, which makes it look

83
00:05:28,210 --> 00:05:30,700
like our trees are fading in.

84
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:38,080
To do this, we need to change this value because currently our ghosting is at 95 and we want to bring

85
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:39,460
it back to zero.

86
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:45,370
So we can just enter in minus three here or whatever looks best for you.

87
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:46,990
So let's give this a test.

88
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:52,360
You can see that our trees now look like they are slowly appearing into view from the mist.

89
00:05:52,660 --> 00:05:58,360
And yes, it's a very subtle effect, but it just makes our game that much better.

90
00:05:58,870 --> 00:06:00,100
So that's it for now.

91
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:05,110
If you can see any other areas where you think the graphics can be improved, I encourage you to give

92
00:06:05,110 --> 00:06:07,060
it a go and see what you can do.

